P4-T2 — audio-volume backup / restore round trip, with erasure re-application
specs/dr-rehearsal/plan.md §P4-T2

WHAT THIS IS
  The hazard (research.md §8.4): restoring a pre-erasure backup RESURRECTS audio a learner asked to
  have deleted. The deletion request stays satisfied in the database; only the bytes come back, and
  nothing anywhere reports it.

  This drill is the pre-erasure backup being restored on purpose, with the re-application step doing
  its job. It is NOT a unit test with a seeded row: ml-inference stored the chunks through its real
  audio path, platform-api's real POST /v1/privacy/delete erased them and wrote privacy_jobs, and
  the two committed scripts did the backup and the restore.

  learner-1 erases after the backup. learner-2 is the CONTROL — without a file that MUST come back,
  "the erased file is absent" is also true of a restore that restored nothing.

HOW TO RE-RUN
  Start ml-inference with AUDIO_STORAGE_DIR set to a scratch volume and platform-api pointed at it,
  then: backup-audio.sh -> POST /v1/privacy/delete -> restore-audio.sh -> compare.
  psql is not on this host (Postgres runs in Docker), so PSQL_BIN="docker exec -i qrai-staging-pg psql".

KNOWN CEILING
  Re-application iterates every key in every privacy_jobs delete row (2000+ in this long-lived dev
  database, from months of test runs) and stats each against the restored tree. Correct, and O(keys)
  per restore. If a real deployment ever reaches a scale where that matters, scope the query by
  tenant or by the backup's own timestamp — the keys are already indexed by neither today.

